#lang rhombus/and_meta
// Generated by glide-pptx from eggcc2.key.
// 40 slides at 1920.0 x 1080.0 pt.
//
// Every slide_N is a pict, and every `at` carries a tag. Open this with
// `raco glide` and what you drag in the editor is written back here --
// nothing else rewrites this file.

import:
  lib("glide-pptx/runtime.rhm") open
  // `fade_in` for the staged reveals -- the same helper the ICFP talk uses.
  "slidehelpers.rhm" open
  // our own magic move: `Frame` and `magic_slide`, which animate a slide from what
  // is on it rather than from two finished pictures. See magicmove.rhm for why the
  // pict library's own `magic_move` cannot do this job.
  "magicmove.rhm" open
  // Prefixed, not `open`: rhombus/pict and the runtime above both export
  // `blank`, and opening both would make that name ambiguous.
  pict as pc
  // `current_font` is pict/text's, which `pict` does not re-export
  pict/text as text
  draw

export:
  slide_width
  slide_height
  all_slides
  slide_1
  slide_2
  slide_12
  slide_20
  slide_update
  slide_egraph
  slide_rue
  slide_fork
  slide_34
  slide_36
  slide_37
  slide_38
  slide_39

def slide_width = 1920.0
def slide_height = 1080.0

// ------------------------------------------------------------------------ faces
// The deck is set in three faces, and every run names one of these rather than a
// typeface, so the scheme is one edit and a fourth face cannot creep in unnamed:
//
//   `prose_font`  titles, captions, callouts -- anything read as English
//   `code_font`   the code boxes, and the e-graph node labels, which are the same
//                 program terms drawn as a graph
//   `math_font`   Greek, primes and the rewrite rules -- the notation, not the code
//
// `code_font` is the theme default too, so a run naming no typeface gets it. That
// default is why the Greek has to be said out loud: PT Mono has no Greek at all,
// so every unnamed sigma in this file was drawn by whatever racket/draw reached
// for instead -- Noto Sans on this machine, something else on a Mac. Naming the
// face is the difference between choosing one and being handed one.
def prose_font = "Helvetica Neue"
def code_font = "PT Mono"
def math_font = "Times New Roman"

// The e-graph node labels get their own name because which face they want is a
// judgement rather than a fact. They follow the notation: they sit beside the
// rewrite rules, they hold the same Greek and primes, and PT Mono draws `0` with a
// slash through it and gives `1` a flag, which is wrong for a term in a graph.
// Setting this to `code_font` draws them as the code boxes instead -- but then every
// sigma needs an exception, because PT Mono has no Greek at all.
def node_font = math_font

current_default_font(code_font)

// ------------------------------------------------------------------------ fonts
// racket/draw substitutes silently when a family is missing, and a substitute
// with different metrics is exactly what goes wrong here: line spacing in this
// runtime is a percentage of the *font size*, so the leading does not move when
// the face does -- only the substitute's ink height does. With Noto Sans
// standing in for Helvetica Neue, the 116pt title's two lines overlapped by
// 5.6pt and ran together.
//
// So name every family the deck asks for and refuse to run on the wrong one. A
// family may declare a stand-in, for the case where it cannot be installed at
// all (Helvetica Neue is Apple's); the stand-in has to be installed, and the
// name has to actually resolve to it, which is checked by measuring -- a font
// reports the face that was asked for, not the one that was drawn.
//
// `fonts` is a folder beside this file. Anything in it is loaded before a
// single glyph is measured, so a deck can carry the faces it is set in rather
// than hope for them: `raco glide fonts talk.rhm` puts them there.
register_fonts("fonts")
//
// One entry per face the deck is set in. Two entries used to be here that were not
// faces: Medium is a weight of the sans that Keynote wrote out as a family of its
// own, and the single Arial run was on an empty string. Both are gone rather than
// checked.
check_fonts(
  [code_font],
  [prose_font, "Nimbus Sans"],                // Helvetica clone, Helvetica metrics
  [math_font, "Tinos"]                        // metric-compatible with Times
)

// Images sit next to this file, so the program travels as a folder.
def media = media_lookup("media")

// The runtime builds Racket picts, but rhombus/pict's animation works on its own
// `Pict`. `Pict.from_handle` is glide-pptx's own bridge between the two -- its
// README calls it exactly that.
// A Racket pict from the runtime crosses over; a `Pict` is already across.
fun as_pict(q): if q is_a pc.Pict | q | pc.Pict.from_handle(q)

// A slide that was several: `base_canvas` is what shows before the first click,
// and each stage fades in over everything already on screen, one click apiece.
//
// `wire(p, k)` adds what is drawn *between* elements -- the edges of a graph --
// to the picture at stage `k`: to the base for `k = 0`, and for each later stage
// to a ghost of the base under the stage's own elements, so that an edge can join
// a node the stage adds to one that was there already, and fades in with it.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The talk's sections.
//
// `divider(k)` is the slide between sections: the bar full size in the middle of
// the slide, which then magic-moves up into the top margin. `in_section(k, ...)`
// puts that same bar, at that same size and place, above a content slide -- so the
// move ends exactly where the next slide's header begins and the bar appears to
// stay put across the cut.
//
// The header's discs sit around y 18..50 and the deck's slide titles start at
// y 64, which is what the disc and text scales in `header_layout` are chosen
// against. Retitle a slide higher than that and the two will meet.
def sections = ["Motivate Effectful E-Graphs", "Effectful E-Graphs",
                "A DP Solution", "It Works!"]

fun section_canvas():
  as_pict(slide_canvas(~width: slide_width, ~height: slide_height,
                       ~background: hex("FFFFFF")))

// The outline and the header are the one text on screen for the whole talk, and the
// only text that names no face: `slidehelpers` draws them with `current_font`, whose
// default is slideshow's rather than anything this deck declared or `check_fonts`
// checked. Both entry points are wrapped so that they are built in the prose face,
// which also picks up the numbers in the discs.
fun in_prose_face(mk):
  fun ():
    parameterize { text.current_font: text.current_font() with (name = prose_font) }:
      mk()

fun divider(k :: Int):
  in_prose_face(
    fun ():
      // only the first divider writes the list out; after that the list is familiar
      // and what matters is which disc is lit
      outline_to_header(sections, ~at: k, ~canvas: section_canvas(), ~intro: k == 0))

fun in_section(k :: Int, mk):
  in_prose_face(
    fun ():
      section_header(sections, ~at: k, ~canvas: as_pict(mk())))

fun staged(base_canvas, stages, ~wire: wire = fun (p, k): p):
  def base = wire(as_pict(base_canvas), 0)
  for values(p = base) (st in stages, k in 1..):
    def extra:
      wire(pc.overlay(~horiz: #'left, ~vert: #'top, base.ghost(), as_pict(st)), k)
    fade_in(p, extra, ~horiz: #'left, ~vert: #'top)

// Keynote's blue, which the state parts of the e-graph are drawn in: the state
// nodes' borders and the edges between them. See `chain_wire`.
def keynote_blue = draw.Color(4, 51, 255)

// Keynote's centred one-line text box, which the deck has forty-one of. Every one
// of them spelled out `~anchor: #'center` and `~wrap: #false` again; they say it
// once here instead. `~width` and `~height` stay the size Keynote gave the box --
// the glyphs are free to overflow it, and several of them do, which is Keynote's
// geometry rather than a mistake to correct here.
//
// The name is seven letters to match `textbox`, so that swapping one for the other
// leaves every continuation line in this file at the column shrubbery wants it.
fun linebox(~width: w :: Real, ~height: h :: Real, & paras, ~& kws):
  textbox(~width: w, ~height: h, ~anchor: #'center, ~wrap: #false, & paras, ~& kws)


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Where the `update` example sits: the program down the left, the graph built
// from it down the right, side by side. `slide_update`, `slide_egraph` and
// `slide_rue` all use these, so the code box lands in one place and stays put
// across the cuts.
//
//    fun foo(a, x, b, y, ω):           a  x  b  y      ω
//      (v1, ω1) = update(a, x, ω)            update
//      (v2, ω2) = update(a, x, ω1)                v1     ω1
//      (v3, ω3) = update(b, y, ω2)           update
//      return (v2, ω3)                            v2     ω2
//                                            update
//    the rule, on `slide_rue`                     v3     ω3
//
// Data flows down, and every edge points the way the data goes. The inputs are in
// a row along the top, the `update`s run down the middle, the value each one
// returns sits beside it and the state chain runs down the right. The columns are
// spaced the way they are on purpose: the long edges -- an `update` reaching back
// up to a variable, a state reaching left to the `update` that reads it -- thread
// gaps between boxes, and moving a column puts them through one.
//
// The code and the graph have to fit beside each other, and the graph needs its
// variables left of the `update` column as well as above it, so the three of them
// share the width: the box takes what is left over once the variable row and the
// column block have what they need. At 40pt that is 938 of it, and the graph
// starts 20 further on -- the walk's pointer used to stand between them, and it
// has gone round to the right of the graph so that the two can sit together. At
// 50 -- what Keynote had -- the box alone is 1224 wide, which is why the text
// used to overflow its own border into the graph.
def code_size = 40.0
def code_x = 95.0            // the deck's left margin, where the titles start
def code_y = 240.0
def code_row = 69.0          // a line of the box: 60pt of ink at 40pt, plus leading
def code_inset = 20.0

def chain_row = 92.0
fun chain_y(layer :: Int) :: Real: 250.0 + layer * chain_row
// The column block, whose three spacings are all load-bearing: the ω-to-`update`
// edge threads the gap between the value column and the state column, a few points
// above the value node in that row, so closing that gap up puts the edge through
// the box.
def chain_op = 1398.0        // the update boxes, which are 180.242 wide
def chain_v = 1598.0         // v1 … v3
def chain_s = 1758.0         // ω, ω1 … ω3
// The variables in a row along the top, in the order the signature names them,
// with the incoming state at the end of the row above its own column. They are
// the inputs, so they belong together and above everything that reads them.
//
// The row sits entirely *left* of the `update` column, which is what makes the
// long edges drawable. An `update` far down the chain has to reach a variable
// past every `update` above it, and those are all in one column: from the left
// the edge has clear air, and from directly above it does not. Two of them still
// have to bow out into the empty band below the row -- see `chain_wire`.
//
// The row starts 20 past the code box, which ends at 1033, and is spread to the
// `update` column at 1398 with everything in between: four boxes fed by three
// `update`s from below make a fan, and the wider the row the less of a thicket the
// fan is.
//
// The extraction pointer walks down the far side, right of the state column, not
// between the code and the graph. It is 80 wide, and standing it in there cost the
// slide a lane of white the width of two nodes for the sake of one arrow.
def chain_point_x = 1846.0
fun leaf_x(i :: Int) :: Real: 1053.0 + i * 88.0
// The node the program's two results meet in, one layer below them: `return` is
// what groups the value and the state into the tuple the function hands back, and
// so it is the root the extraction starts from.
def chain_ret = 1620.0

// The rule box on `slide_rue`, under the code it is about. It is scaled to the
// width of the code box, whatever that measures, so the two of them share a right
// edge and the left of the slide reads as one column with the pointer's lane
// beside it, rather than as two boxes with a ragged gap to their right.
def rule_x = 95.0
def rule_y = 600.0           // under the code box, which is now 316 tall

